millennials don't conform to traditional  political stereotypes there are social  liberals and fiscal centrists they're  pro-business and they're pro-government  however their support for government  action has been overblown in the media  let's walk through this first they like  business a majority of Millennials say  they themselves would like to start  their own business one day they're  favorable towards profit and competition  and they strongly prefer a free-market  system over a government managed economy  however Millennials came of a  politically impressionable age during or  shortly after the Great Recession and  the ensuing sluggish economy has left a  third of them under or unemployed and a  third are living at home with their  parents consequently many of them are  uncertain as to whether the free market  system drives their own income mobility  and it's likely that this uncertainty  has bolstered their support for  government action that being said  Millennials also realize that government  comes with its own problems for instance  sixty-six percent say that government is  usually wasteful and inefficient this is  up 20 points from where it stood in 2009  two-thirds also think that government  regulators when they write and enforce  regulations usually favor special  interests over the public interests and  then fully fifty-eight percent of  Millennials say the government agencies  generally abuse their power we also find  that as Millennials make more money and  pay more in taxes their support for  income redistribution steadily declines  for instance among Millennials making  less than twenty thousand dollars a year  a majority support income redistribution  however once Millennials make forty  thousand dollars a year or more support  flips and a majority oppose income  redistribution what these data indicate  is that Millennials become more fiscally  conservative as they age pay more in  taxes and take on more responsibilities  however many of these responsibilities  are being delayed for this cohort we  find that they were getting married  later buying homes later and starting  families when they're older but these  life experiences will eventually occur  and when they do we could find this  cohort to be  supportive of government action than  they were in their youth  you
 

